# ORDER MATTERS: the LAST matching pattern wins. The catch-all goes first so
# the byte-exact rules below can override it. Written the other way round, the
# catch-all silently un-did every `-text` rule and `git check-attr` still said
# `text: auto`.

* text=auto

# The drift boundary hashes EXACT BYTES. Git's line-ending translation changes
# those bytes, so a pod generated on Windows and checked out on Linux hashed
# differently and every published report failed to re-derive. CI on a fresh
# Linux runner is what caught it; the local suite never could, because it only
# ever saw one platform's checkout.
#
# `-text` means: never translate, in either direction. These are artifacts, not
# prose, and a pod is only portable if its bytes survive the trip.
*.jsonl   -text
*.json    -text
*.yaml    -text
*.yml     -text
*.svg     -text
STOMP.md  -text

# Pod CODE is hashed too: `target_sha256`, `tool_sha256_by_name`, and a custom
# scorer's hash all read these bytes. Under `text=auto` a Windows clone with
# autocrlf would receive CRLF, hash differently, and fail to re-derive a report
# that verifies perfectly on Linux. Same reasoning as the artifacts above, and
# the same failure it would produce; found while widening the CRLF guard rather
# than by anyone hitting it.
*.py      -text
